course: chemistry
lesson: 12
title: Fire: what it needs and how to put it out
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: A fire needs three things at once: fuel to burn, heat to keep it going, and oxygen from the air; this is the fire triangle on the poster at school.
explain: Take away any one side and the fire goes out: water takes away heat, a lid or a fire blanket takes away oxygen, and a gap cut in a forest takes away fuel.
explain: The right method matters: water on a burning chip pan turns to steam in an instant and throws burning oil everywhere, so a lid is used instead.
explain: In a real fire the most dangerous thing is usually the smoke, which is why smoke alarms save lives and why the rule is "get out, stay out, call 999" (or 112).
explain: Children's job in a fire is not to fight it but to get out fast and tell a grown-up.
fact: The fire triangle: fire needs fuel, heat and oxygen, and goes out if one is taken away.
fact: A lid on a burning pan cuts off the oxygen; water must never be thrown on burning oil, because it turns to steam and throws the oil out in a fireball.
fact: A fire blanket smothers a small fire by cutting off oxygen; if your own clothes catch fire, the rule is "stop, drop and roll".
fact: Fire extinguishers use water, foam, powder or carbon dioxide; the coloured band shows which fires each one is for.
fact: Water must never be used on an electrical fire, because water carries electricity; carbon dioxide extinguishers push the oxygen away instead.
fact: A smoke alarm senses tiny smoke particles; test it by pressing its button, never with a flame.
fact: Most people who die in house fires are killed by smoke, not flames, often while asleep.
fact: In a fire you leave at once by the safest way, never go back for toys or pets, and call 999 (UK) or 112 (Europe) from outside.
fact: Smoke rises, so the air near the floor is cleaner: crawl low under smoke; feel a door with the back of your hand before opening it, and if it is hot, do not open it.
fact: Smoke is tiny bits of unburnt fuel, ash and gases; wet fuel makes thick smoke, and burning plastic makes especially poisonous smoke.
fact: Water puts out fires mainly by cooling, because it soaks up a lot of heat as it turns to steam.
fact: Wildfires spread fast in dry, hot, windy weather: dry plants are fuel, heat dries the next plants, and wind brings fresh oxygen; firefighters cut gaps to remove fuel.
fact: Some trees and seeds need fire to open their cones and grow.
fact: Hedgehogs sleep in warm, dry heaps in autumn, so bonfire piles should be rebuilt on the day or checked with a torch before lighting.
exercise: What three things make up the fire triangle? | Fuel, heat and oxygen | Wood, matches and wind | Smoke, flames and grey ash | Water, air and a spark
exercise: Why does a lid on a burning pan put the fire out? | It cuts off the oxygen | It cools the oil with metal | It squashes the flames flat | It soaks up all the hot oil
exercise: What is a fire blanket for? | Smothering a small fire by cutting off its oxygen | Keeping everyone warm while they stand outside during a fire drill | Wrapping up hot food | Cleaning up after a fire
exercise: What do the coloured bands on fire extinguishers show? | Which kinds of fire each one is for | How old each extinguisher is and when it must be thrown away | Which classroom it belongs to | How heavy it is
exercise: How does a smoke alarm know there is smoke? | It senses tiny smoke particles in the air | It feels the heat of the flames from far away | It hears the fire crackle | It smells burning just like a nose does
exercise: What does "get out, stay out, call 999" mean in a fire? | Leave at once, never go back in, then phone for help | Fight the fire yourself first, then call for help when it is out | Hide under your bed and call | Find your toys, then get out
exercise: Why does bonfire smoke make you cough? | Smoke is tiny bits of unburnt fuel, ash and gases | Smoke is made of pure steam, which is far too hot for your throat | Smoke is clean air that is too hot | Smoke has extra oxygen in it
exercise: Which side of the fire triangle does a firefighter's water mainly take away? | Heat | The fuel | Oxygen | Smoke
exercise: Why does wind make a forest fire worse? | It brings fresh oxygen and carries the heat on | It cools the flames down so they creep along the ground | It blows the fuel away | It brings rain clouds
exercise: Why should a bonfire pile be checked before lighting? | Hedgehogs and other animals may be sleeping inside | The pile could have too much oxygen hidden inside it | The wood might be too dry | Firefighters must count the sticks
exercise: Why must water never go on a burning chip pan? | It turns to steam and throws burning oil everywhere | It makes the oil colder far too fast, so the pan cracks in half | It makes the fire smell bad | It turns the oil into ice
exercise: Why should you crawl low under smoke? | Smoke rises, so the air near the floor is cleaner | The floor is cooler for your knees and hands | Fire cannot see you down there | Smoke always falls to the floor first
exercise: Why should plastic never go on a bonfire? | It makes especially poisonous smoke | It puts the whole bonfire out | It turns into water | It burns too slowly to matter
exercise: Why do firefighters sometimes cut gaps in a forest? | To take away the fuel the fire needs | To let more wind blow through the trees | To make a road for the lorries only | To plant new trees there
test: Your mate says: "Chip pan's on fire, chuck a bucket of water on it!" What should happen instead? | Never water: cover it with a lid and get out | Throw the water quickly, because water always puts out every fire | Blow on it hard | Carry the pan outside
test: "why can't you put water on an electric fire, water puts everything out" Best answer? | Water carries electricity and could give a deadly shock | Water makes electric fires much bigger and hotter | Electric fires are too cold for water | Water turns into oil near plugs
test: Your friend tests the smoke alarm by holding a lighter under it. What should he do instead? | Press the alarm's test button | Blow smoke at it from a candle | Hit it hard with a broom handle | Spray it with water from a bottle
test: In a cartoon, a boy runs back into a burning house to fetch his teddy. What is the right rule? | Get out, stay out; never go back in for toys or pets | Go back in quickly if it is a favourite toy and the stairs are clear | Go back if you hold your breath | Go back if the fire looks small
test: Before opening a door in a fire drill, why do you feel it with the back of your hand? | A hot door means fire behind it, so keep it shut | To check that it is not locked | To clean your hand | To warm up your hand
test: Your clothes caught fire in a story. What does the hero do? | Stop, drop and roll | Run as fast as possible | Wave his arms to blow it out | Jump up and down to shake it off
test: "who even dies of smoke, it's the flames that get ya" What is the truth? | Most house-fire deaths are from smoke, often while asleep | Smoke is harmless to breathe, and only the flames themselves can hurt you | Nobody has ever died from smoke | Only flames can travel through houses
test: What would happen if you took away the fuel from a campfire? | It would go out when the fuel already burning is used up | It would keep burning forever on the air alone | It would get bigger | It would turn into smoke only
test: Your brother says: "All fire is bad for forests." Is that the whole story? | Some trees and seeds need fire to open and grow | Yes, no plant at all can survive a forest fire | Fire only helps forests in winter | Forests cannot burn
test: Why would a hedgehog choose a bonfire heap to sleep in? | It is a warm, dry heap, good for winter sleep | It likes the smell of smoke | It wants to stay up and watch the fireworks | Hedgehogs love the heat from flames
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the requirements of combustion? | Fuel, heat and oxygen must be present simultaneously | Fuel alone suffices in the absence of air, provided it be heated | Heat and water must be present together | Oxygen alone suffices in the absence of fuel
exam: Identify the principal reason for which water shall not be applied to burning cooking oil. | It is instantly converted to steam and projects the burning oil outward | It cools the oil insufficiently to have any effect | It reacts chemically with the oil to form a poisonous and flammable liquid | It increases the supply of fuel to the fire
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established procedure in the event of a domestic fire. | Evacuate without delay, refrain from re-entering, and summon the emergency services | Remain within the dwelling until the fire is extinguished | Retrieve personal belongings and pets from every room prior to evacuation, then summon assistance | Attempt to extinguish every fire personally
exam: It is given that a wildfire burns in dry, hot and windy weather. Identify the principal reason for which the wind aggravates it. | The wind supplies fresh oxygen and carries heat to further vegetation | The wind cools the flames and slows their progress | The wind removes the fuel from the path of the fire | The wind brings moisture to the vegetation
